1. Observations

Initial Observation: The control description reads as a control activity, not a condition statement. A named actor (AzP Partnerships Director) performs defined actions (development and annual review) at a stated frequency (annual) on a named object (AzP Intermediaries Strategy). This is a factual finding; the risk-component mapping follows.

Risk-component mapping:

- Sales steering strategy (existence) — Appears to address. The control describes the development and annual review of a formally documented strategy covering "high-level principles and approach for managing the agent distribution channel." The description confirms a strategy document exists and is periodically refreshed.

- Target setting framework — Not addressed. The Risk Description names "target setting framework" as a distinct component alongside the sales steering strategy. The control description does not reference target setting, performance metrics, KPIs, or any framework for establishing quantitative or qualitative targets.

- Identification and appointment of new agents aligned to growth objectives — Not clearly addressed. The description references "intermediary channel growth" and "managing the agent distribution channel," but names no mechanism, criteria, or process for identifying or appointing new agents. Strategic direction is referenced; the operational pathway to agent identification and appointment is not described.

- FCA regulatory requirements — Not addressed. The Risk Description explicitly names FCA regulatory requirements as a condition for agent identification and appointment. The control description contains no reference to FCA requirements, regulatory compliance, or regulatory alignment.

Scope-mismatch observation: A portion of the control description — the three strategic pillars (Simpler, Smarter, Stronger) and alignment with AzUK/AzP Retail strategies — describes strategic architecture that may or may not relate to the specific risk components above. Without further definition of what these pillars encompass, their relevance to the stated risk cannot be confirmed from the description alone. If a fuller catalogue definition of B.1 exists that elaborates on these pillars, several of these observations may already be addressed in content not reflected in this description.

2. Control Type Consideration

The control is classified as preventive, which is logically consistent — establishing and maintaining a formal strategy document in advance should prevent the scenario where no defined strategy exists. As a yearly control, there is a 12-month window between reviews during which the strategy may become misaligned with evolving business conditions, growth targets, or regulatory changes. Whether this annual frequency creates an exposure window the Audit Objective would be concerned with is an observation for the auditor to weigh, not a determination made here.

3. Specificity Assessment (4W+1H)

Per the NFRM Functional Rule, Section C.IV.2: a control description should specify what is monitored, why the control is necessary, who performs or owns it, when and how frequently it is carried out, and how it is carried out including the evidence generated.

- What (subject monitored) — Partially Stated. The control names the document (AzP Intermediaries Strategy) and states it defines "high-level principles and approach," but does not specify what specific elements within the strategy are subject to the annual review (e.g., whether growth targets, agent appointment criteria, or regulatory compliance elements are within scope).

- Why (linkage to the risk) — Partially Stated. Alignment with AzUK and AzP Retail strategies is referenced, and driving alignment between AzP Partnerships team objectives and broader business objectives is stated. However, the explicit linkage to the risk's stated failure pathway — particularly the target setting framework and FCA regulatory requirements — is not articulated.

- Who (actor/owner) — Present. The AzP Partnerships Director is named as the owner of the development and annual review.

- When (frequency/trigger) — Present. Annual review is stated.

- How (method and evidence) — Absent. The description does not state how the development or review is carried out (e.g., what inputs are considered, what review steps are performed, what governance or approval mechanism is required). No evidence type is named (e.g., documented sign-off, board or committee approval minutes, version-controlled strategy document). This matters for future DET/OET readiness even though DET and OET review is not yet available in this tool.

4. Suggested Refinement(s)

Addressing the observations above would require consideration of the following, framed as options for the auditor:

- Target setting framework: Specify whether the AzP Intermediaries Strategy includes or is complemented by a target setting framework, or whether target setting is addressed by a separate control. If included, the control description should state this explicitly.

- FCA regulatory alignment: Specify whether FCA regulatory requirements for agent identification and appointment are considered within the strategy or its review process. If this is addressed elsewhere in the control framework, cross-referencing would clarify the coverage.

- Agent identification and appointment: Clarify whether the strategy defines criteria or processes for identifying and appointing new agents aligned to growth objectives, or whether this operational mechanism is addressed by a downstream control.

- How (method and evidence): Describe the review process — what inputs are considered, what governance or approval mechanism applies, and what evidence is generated (e.g., documented approval, committee minutes, version-controlled strategy document with sign-off).

- What (scope of review): Specify which elements within the strategy document are subject to the annual review, to distinguish between a full strategic refresh and a confirmation of continued applicability.

5. Methodology Compliance & References

Per Allianz UK SAM v2 - Final (02-02-2025).docx, Section D.VII (Controls): "Control descriptions should articulate what is done, when it is done, who is doing it, how it manages the risk and how it is evidenced." The control description partially meets this standard — it states who (AzP Partnerships Director) and when (annually), partially states what (the strategy document, but not the specific elements reviewed), does not clearly articulate how the control manages the risk for all stated risk components, and does not describe how the control activity is evidenced.

Per the NFRM Functional Rule, Section C.IV.2: the 4W+1H standard requires What, Why, Who, When, and How (including evidence generated). Two elements are Present (Who, When), two are Partially Stated (What, Why), and one is Absent (How).

